# Freeze-backdrop source corpus — tests/harness/check_freeze_backdrop.sh
#
# One windowed replay per row, to the frame limit, counting the composed
# presents that qualified as the freeze backdrop's source frame.
#
#   <bk2> | <frame-limit> | <launch tokens> | world_min=<n> presents_min=<n>
#
# `launch` takes the presentation-corpus convention: a whitespace-separated
# token spelled KEY=VALUE with an upper-case key is an environment entry,
# every other token a command-line argument. OPENPETE_TERRAIN_CACHE_FORCE=1
# makes the retained terrain cache initialise on a software rasteriser, which
# is what puts the source predicate's terrain clause under test at all.
#
# pause-menu-text-moby-swim enters the pause menu around VBL 1750 in artisans.
# Under --vertex-precision integer the retained cache is unusable, so the
# world's terrain reaches the present as scene triangles instead of retained
# state; a source predicate that reads only the retained state then rejects
# every present of the run and the pause backdrop is a flat green fill.
# Measured over four runs of one build: 83, 25, 23, 82 qualifying presents of
# 173, 70, 72, 172 composed. The floor sits at a third of the lowest of those,
# because the count is proportional to the host's present throughput over the
# run and the defect it separates reads zero at any throughput.
pause-menu-text-moby-swim.bk2 | 1756 | --vertex-precision integer OPENPETE_TERRAIN_CACHE_FORCE=1 | world_min=8 presents_min=10
# The same movie with the retained cache usable: the control leg, which the
# defect never reached. It presents fewer frames than the integer leg, since
# the retained path costs more per frame on a software rasteriser, and its
# floor is set from its own measurements: 7, 7, 12, 12 of 30, 29, 78, 78.
pause-menu-text-moby-swim.bk2 | 1756 | OPENPETE_TERRAIN_CACHE_FORCE=1 | world_min=3 presents_min=10
